= StructuredQuote Template Documentation =

== Overview ==
The StructuredQuote template is used to create stylized quotations with optional attribution. It generates a table-based layout with customizable appearance and content.

== Usage ==
<pre>
{{StructuredQuote
| text = The quote text goes here
| speaker = Optional: Name of the speaker
| receiver = Optional: Name of the person being addressed
| attribution = Optional: Additional attribution details
| source = Optional: Source of the quote
}}
</pre>

=== Output ===
{{StructuredQuote
| text = The quote text goes here
| speaker = Optional: Name of the speaker
| receiver = Optional: Name of the person being addressed
| attribution = Optional: Additional attribution details
| source = Optional: Source of the quote
}}

== Parameters ==
# `text` (required): The main text of the quote.
# `speaker` (optional): The name of the person who said the quote.
# `receiver` (optional): The name of the person to whom the quote was directed.
# `attribution` (optional): Additional attribution information.
# `source` (optional): The source of the quote.

== Notes ==
* The template automatically adjusts its layout based on which optional parameters are provided.
* If any of the optional parameters (speaker, receiver, attribution, or source) are provided, an additional row is added to the table for attribution.
* The template is designed to be responsive and should adapt to different screen sizes.

== Example ==
<pre>
{{StructuredQuote
| text = To be, or not to be, that is the question.
| speaker = Hamlet
| source = Hamlet, Act 3, Scene 1
}}
</pre>
This will produce a styled quote box with the text from Hamlet, attributed to the character and sourced from the play.


=== Output ===
{{StructuredQuote
| text = To be, or not to be, that is the question.
| speaker = Hamlet
| source = Hamlet, Act 3, Scene 1
}}